<title>USA: Table of most affordable U.S. housing by regions.</title>
<headline>Table of most affordable U.S. housing by regions.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-07-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>The National Association of Home Builders released its housing affordability index for the first quarter Thursday, showing the Midwest city of Kokomo, Ind., at the top of a national list.</p>
<p>The index provides a measure of the relative degree of affordability of homes sold in 190 metropolitan areas across the country, taking into account selling prices, incomes, taxes and costs of home maintenance in each region.</p>
<p>Listed below are the top ten most affordable regions, with the most affordable first, followed by the top ten most costly regions, with the most costly first.</p>
<p>10 Most Affordable   Share of Homes     Median    Median sales</p>
<p>Metro areas	    Affordable for     Income    price</p>
<p>			   Median Income	(000s)    (000s)</p>
<p>Kokomo, Ind.-1	     89.5		46.9	 85</p>
<p>Baton Rouge, La.-2	 88.9		42.6	 85</p>
<p>Beaumont-Port Arthur,</p>
<p>   Texas-2		   86.1		40.9	 72</p>
<p>Elmira, N.Y.-1	     86.1		38.8	 60</p>
<p>Wilmington-Newark,</p>
<p>   Del.-Md.-2		86.0		57.6	119</p>
<p>Rockford, Ill.-2	   85.3		47.1	 83</p>
<p>Davenport-Moline-Rock</p>
<p>   Island, Iowa-Ill.-2   84.8		43.6	 65</p>
<p>Racine, Wisc.-1	    84.6		49.1	 80</p>
<p>Lima, Ohio-1		 84.4		42.3	 73</p>
<p>Kansas City, Mo.-Kan.-3  84.1		50.2	 91</p>
<p>10 Least Affordable  Share of Homes     Median    Median sales</p>
<p>Metro areas	    Affordable for     Income    price</p>
<p>			   Median Income	(000s)    (000s)</p>
<p>San Francisco-3	    23.0		64.4	288</p>
<p>Portland-Vancouver,</p>
<p>   Ore.-Wash.-3	    30.8		46.3	147</p>
<p>Laredo, Texas-1	    34.0		27.3	 90</p>
<p>Santa Cruz-Watsonville,</p>
<p>   Calif.-1		  35.4		55.2	217</p>
<p>Santa Rosa, Calif.-2     35.6		51.3	190</p>
<p>Salinas, Calif.-2	  36.8		45.6	171</p>
<p>Provo-Orem, Utah-2	 38.0		41.8	144</p>
<p>San Jose, Calif.-3	 39.4		70.2	260</p>
<p>Honolulu-2		   40.9		57.9	226</p>
<p>New York-3		   41.2		47.3	170</p>
<p>Notes: 1 - population below 250,000.</p>
<p>	 2 - population of 250,000 to one million.</p>
<p>	 3 - population more than one million.</p>
